The inlined tree is not byte-identical to the current branch. Its last detailed
audit was against source commit 0648c56 on 2026-07-25, before the branch tip
above. That audit found 33 of 34 files pair up (python.rs is dropped). 23ce2cb
adds only the GameCube FST read in disc/reader.rs (encounter/nod#29), mirrored
here verbatim. The audit describes that source snapshot; it does not certify
the current branch tip. Its differences fell into these categories:

  formatting     nod's rustfmt.toml is nightly (fn_single_line,
                 use_small_heuristics = "Max", imports_granularity); this repo
                 uses stable defaults. This is most of the raw diff - io/wia.rs
                 is 250 differing lines that collapse to 0 once both sides are
                 formatted the same way.
  adaptation     `crate::` -> `crate::nod::` and doc paths -> `rom_weaver_containers::nod::`;
                 lib.rs -> mod.rs; Python bindings and the OpenSSL backend
                 dropped (util/aes.rs, util/digest.rs - the largest real
                 category at ~174 lines); compression feature gates resolved to
                 the pinned set; extra crate-level #![allow] for workspace lints.
  dead code      Items upstream carries under #[allow(unused)] are deleted here
                 rather than gated: preloader's group_hashes/GroupHashes,
                 request_rx/stat_tx, last_adjust and its commented-out adaptive
                 thread block, thread_id, shutdown(); nkit's ExtraData and
                 IndexFile variants.
  bug fixes      Fixes found by the rom-weaver test sweep, developed in fork
                 commit 8dd55ec (rebased as a3d9f71) and mirrored here. bzip2
                 stores an incompressible block raw instead of
                 failing (util/compress.rs); read_dol floors the DOL size at
                 the header and saturates the section sums (disc/gcn.rs);
                 Fst::new computes the string table base in u64 (disc/fst.rs);
                 FstBuilder pads a Wii FST to four bytes so the boot header can
                 describe it (disc/fst.rs); the decrypted-partition guess makes
                 data_end_sector absolute (disc/reader.rs); three println!
                 calls became tracing (io/gcz.rs, build/gc.rs). These ARE
                 functional divergences - the only ones, and they are
                 candidates for upstreaming.
  clippy         Two refactors that exist only here, replacing upstream #[allow]s
                 and worth upstreaming: CheckBlockOptions in disc/writer.rs
                 (replaces #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]; call sites in
                 io/ciso.rs and io/wbfs.rs) and Window::is_empty in util/mod.rs
                 (replaces #[allow(clippy::len_without_is_empty)]).

So: diff against the fork expecting differences, and re-sync deliberately. If a
diff ever shows something outside these categories, it is a real divergence.
